# COMPONENT  kube_apiserver
#
# Ten repeat runs of this sweep reduced to mean +/- sd of the fleet-wide memory
# working set for ONE component, one row per RPS step, both arms side by side.
# Written by cld_pool_sn_memory.py; read that file's docstring for the why.
#
# SOURCE     the <run-dir>/plot_sn_memory.dat files beside this one -- the same fleet-wide
#            `totals.memory.<component>` scalars plot_sn_resources.py already
#            took from <run-dir>/<arm>/metrics_<rps>.json and converted from
#            bytes to MiB, each one a Prometheus instant vector already averaged
#            over its load step's window. This file re-reads those .dat files
#            rather than the raw JSON, so a row here is exactly the ten per-run
#            rows averaged, with no second derivation that could drift from them.
# TRANSFORM  MEAN ACROSS RUNS, not a sum: a working set in MiB is intensive, so
#            the ten runs measure the same level ten times and the estimator is
#            their mean. (cld_pool_sn_latency.py sums its request counts because
#            those are extensive -- these files merge differently on purpose.)
#            <arm>_sd is the SAMPLE standard deviation (n-1) of those same ten
#            values -- the run-to-run spread left by an HPA feedback loop
#            settling on a noisy signal. It is N/A where only one run measured
#            the step, because one observation has no spread to report.
#            ratio is RECOMPUTED FROM THE POOLED MEANS (Mazu/Istio), never
#            averaged from the ten per-run ratio columns: mean(a/b) is not
#            mean(a)/mean(b), and the per-run ratios diverge worst exactly where
#            the denominator is smallest. N/A unless both arms have a mean and
#            the Istio mean is non-zero.
#            N/A rows in a per-run file are DROPPED from that cell, never read as
#            0 -- n_Istio says how many runs are actually under each mean.
# CAVEAT     read a mean next to its sd, never on its own: a gap between the arms
#            at one step means nothing until it is larger than the spread of the
#            runs behind it.
#            A FLEET-WIDE WORKING SET LARGELY TRACKS THE POD COUNT. Every replica
#            carries a baseline RSS whether or not it is serving, so this number
#            climbs when HPA adds pods even at flat load, and an arm that ran
#            more replicas shows a larger working set for that reason alone --
#            more so than for CPU, which at least scales with work done. Read
#            this file next to plot_sn_pod_growth_pooled_<arm>.dat before calling
#            a gap a memory regression: it may be the same memory per pod spread
#            over more pods.
#            Everything the per-run file warns about still applies -- in
#            particular these are FLEET-WIDE sums, not per-pod averages, because
#            under HPA the replica count differs between arms and steps and a
#            per-pod average flatters whichever arm ran more replicas.
# NOTE       kube_apiserver is a CLUSTER-WIDE background cost. It is reported for
#            completeness but, unlike the other four components, is not
#            attributable to this workload.
# UNITS      memory (working set) in MiB throughout -- never GiB for the larger
#            components, so every row stays directly comparable.
# LAYOUT     WHITESPACE-ALIGNED, and every cell is non-empty -- a missing
#            value is the literal N/A, never a blank. gnuplot collapses a run of
#            spaces into one separator, so a blank cell would silently shift
#            every column after it into the wrong place (the tab-separated
#            per-run .dat files can leave a field empty; these cannot).
#            one row per RPS step; one file per component
#            (plot_sn_memory_pooled_<component>.dat). The component is named in
#            the header block above, and the files are otherwise identical in
#            shape. Columns 1-3 are rps and the per-arm means, so a plot
#            reading `using 1:2:3` gets the arms and nothing else.
# rps   Istio    Mazu     Istio_sd  Mazu_sd  ratio_Mazu_over_Istio  n_Istio  n_Mazu
  100   1425.68  1393.15  23.86     28.49    0.977                  10       10
  200   1422.16  1369.83  21.38     31.56    0.963                  10       10
  400   1412.24  1371.10  29.82     40.19    0.971                  10       10
  600   1426.64  1380.83  29.06     37.07    0.968                  10       10
  800   1418.64  1405.68  32.52     33.81    0.991                  10       10
  1000  1439.71  1404.58  29.08     35.32    0.976                  10       10
  1200  1413.02  1378.83  30.36     30.55    0.976                  10       10
